+## 6. What Sarvam should use first
+
+Start with a small set that addresses the current acceptance gaps in the companion advisory.
+
+| Priority | References | Bounded work for Sutram | Evidence that makes the work acceptable |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| **1. Reliable baseline and examples** | Vedic, Vāk, VakyaLang | One canonical build path, accurate feature/profile status and examples that compile and run under the documented command. | Exact source/build identity, clean reproduction, observed exit/stdout/stderr and separate PASS/FAIL/SKIP/NOT_RUN reporting. |
+| **2. Precise language and diagnostics** | Sanskritam, Vāk, karaka-core, Devgoyal's IDE, Sanskriti | Grammar/semantics clauses, stable diagnostic IDs and optional views into actual compiler stages. | Positive and negative conformance cases, correct source locations through imports and equivalent behavior across language packs. |
+| **3. Resource lifetime** | Rust, Zig | Finish the staged `kosh` reclamation work with an explicit owner/alias/failure contract. | Generated Sutram A/B programs, repeated-growth observations, allocation-failure behavior, regression evidence and separate platform acceptance. |
+| **4. Real modules and maintainable native output** | chibicc, Sassembly, LLVM/QBE | Define the object/symbol/relocation contract and compile actual Sutram modules independently. Consider further internal structure only where needed. | Two separately compiled `.sm` units linked and executed, visibility/error cases, ABI observations and scale beyond current fixed limits. |
+| **5. Measured usability and performance** | OM Lang, Vāc, Vedic, Smrti, GCC | Matched multilingual examples, a tested newcomer workflow and representative correctness/performance workloads. | Explicit input/output specifications, environment/flags, repeated measurements and separate usability observations. |
+| **Later research** | PaniniVM, KCC, Vidyut, sanskrit_parser, Heritage | Explore grammatical argument roles or an optional language assistant after the deterministic core is stable. | Bounded supported grammar, ambiguity rules and an evaluation against simpler existing syntax. |
+
+Windows native execution and a Sutram-generated freestanding demonstration retain their own acceptance gates in the companion advisory. Neither follows automatically from studying another project's platform claims.
+
+### A repeatable adoption process
+
+1. **Choose one problem.** For example: imported-file error locations, growth invalidating aliases, or two modules that must link.
+2. **Read one relevant implementation or specification.** Record the upstream revision and what was actually inspected. Check the applicable license before copying source.
+3. **Write a Sutram contract.** Define accepted syntax/inputs, observable behavior, error cases, compatibility and target/profile limits.
+4. **Implement within the accepted architecture.** Preserve pure NASM and existing accepted behavior. Introduce a new subsystem only when the contract needs it.
+5. **Verify generated programs.** Include negative cases, boundary cases and regressions. Keep helper tests and full compiler/program acceptance distinct.
+6. **Measure the desired improvement.** Choose a metric suited to the claim: error-location correctness, module scale, retained allocation, binary size, execution time or completion of a first-program task.
+7. **Publish evidence and synchronize documentation.** Give the owner exact revisions, commands, results and remaining limits. An uploaded plan or source file is not proof that the behavior has shipped.
+
+## 7. What this handoff does and does not establish
+
+This is an actionable reading and development catalogue. It does not establish that Sutram is already faster, safer or more mature than the listed projects. The defensible opportunity is to combine native output, a precise multilingual core and dependable tools, then demonstrate the claimed advantages with reproducible evidence.
+
+All external statuses are based on material consulted on 2026-10-17 and can change. Papers, project self-descriptions, package metadata and repository source have different evidentiary weight. Recheck the specific source before committing engineering time.
+
+This delivery adds documentation only. It does not train a model, change the compiler, run a benchmark, issue a new numbered implementation round, or establish that Sarvam has read or accepted the recommendations. The sender will verify the uploaded files before reporting completion to the owner.
